<section class="container content-section"><div class="content-narrow"><h2>How <?=htmlspecialchars($tool['title'])?> works</h2>
<?php if($slug==='unicode-to-non-unicode'): ?><p>Non-Unicode text is not one universal format. Older desktop-publishing fonts use their own keyboard and glyph mappings, so the correct target must be selected before conversion. This tool currently supports five specific profiles: Preeti for Nepali, Bamini for Tamil, Bijoy / SutonnyMJ for Bengali, FM Abhaya for Sinhala, and AnmolLipi for Punjabi.</p><h2>How to convert Unicode to a legacy font</h2><ol><li>Select the exact target font profile from the dropdown.</li><li>Paste Unicode text written in the corresponding language.</li><li>Convert the text and copy or download the result.</li><li>In your destination editor, apply the matching legacy font to the converted text.</li><li>Check vowel signs, conjuncts, punctuation and spacing before using the result.</li></ol><h2>Supported target encodings</h2><ul><li><a href="/unicode-to-preeti/">Preeti</a> — Nepali / Devanagari</li><li><a href="/unicode-to-bamini/">Bamini</a> — Tamil</li><li><a href="/unicode-to-bijoy/">Bijoy / SutonnyMJ</a> — Bengali</li><li><a href="/unicode-to-fm-abaya/">FM Abhaya</a> — Sinhala</li><li><a href="/unicode-to-anmollipi/">AnmolLipi</a> — Punjabi / Gurmukhi</li></ul><h2>Why font selection matters</h2><p>Legacy encodings are font-specific. A string created for one font may look like random letters or symbols in another. DevLys, Chanakya and AnmolLipi are separate profiles and are not included in this selector yet. They will be added only after their exact mappings and representative samples are verified. Keep the original Unicode text and proofread the converted output.</p><h2>Related converters</h2><div class="link-pills"><a href="/unicode-to-preeti/">Unicode to Preeti</a><a href="/unicode-to-bamini/">Unicode to Bamini</a><a href="/unicode-to-bijoy/">Unicode to Bijoy</a><a href="/unicode-to-fm-abaya/">Unicode to FM Abhaya</a></div>
<?php if($slug==='anmollipi-to-unicode'||$slug==='unicode-to-anmollipi'): ?><p>AnmolLipi is a legacy Gurmukhi font encoding used in older Punjabi documents and Gurbani software. The underlying text is stored in a legacy ASCII-style representation rather than Unicode Gurmukhi. This converter supports the documented AnmolLipi / GurmukhiAkhar ASCII profile and converts it to Unicode Gurmukhi, or back to legacy keystrokes.</p><h2>How to use the AnmolLipi converter</h2><ol><li>Choose AnmolLipi → Unicode for text copied from a document that uses the compatible legacy encoding.</li><li>Choose Unicode → AnmolLipi to prepare Gurmukhi text for a compatible legacy-font workflow.</li><li>Paste your text and select the conversion direction.</li><li>Convert a short sample first, then check sihari placement, subjoined letters, nasal marks and punctuation.</li><li>For Unicode-to-AnmolLipi output, apply a compatible AnmolLipi or GurmukhiAkhar font in your destination editor.</li></ol><h2>AnmolLipi conversion example</h2><p>The legacy sequence <code>gurU</code> converts to <strong>ਗੁਰੂ</strong>. This is a quick check for the shared Gurmukhi legacy ASCII profile.</p><h2>Important AnmolLipi notes</h2><p>This mapping follows the legacy ASCII profile documented for AnmolLipi and GurmukhiAkhar. Other Punjabi legacy fonts such as Asees, Joy, Satluj and GurbaniLipi may use different mappings and are not automatically compatible. Some historical glyph distinctions, including open-top yayya variants and certain nasalized vowel combinations, cannot be preserved exactly when converted to standard Unicode. The legacy ASCII format is also not fully reversible for every character sequence. Keep the original text and proofread important documents.</p><h2>Related Punjabi and Unicode tools</h2><div class="link-pills"><a href="/anmollipi-to-unicode/">AnmolLipi to Unicode</a><a href="/unicode-to-anmollipi/">Unicode to AnmolLipi</a><a href="/unicode-normalizer/">Unicode Normalizer</a><a href="/utf8-encoder-decoder/">UTF-8 Encoder &amp; Decoder</a></div>
<?php if($slug==='fm-abaya-to-unicode'||$slug==='unicode-to-fm-abaya'): ?><p>FM Abhaya is a legacy Sinhala font encoding used in older Sinhala documents and desktop-publishing workflows. The text may be stored as Latin characters and symbols that display as Sinhala only when the matching font is applied. This converter changes between supported FM Abhaya keystrokes and standard Unicode Sinhala.</p><h2>How to use the FM Abhaya converter</h2><ol><li>Choose FM Abhaya → Unicode for text copied from a document formatted with an FM Abhaya-compatible font.</li><li>Choose Unicode → FM Abhaya when preparing Sinhala text for a legacy workflow that requires this encoding.</li><li>Paste the text and select the conversion direction.</li><li>Convert a short sample first, then check vowel signs, joined letters, punctuation and spacing.</li></ol><h2>FM Abhaya conversion example</h2><p>The legacy sequence <code>› ,xldj ,iaik rgls'</code> converts to <strong>ශ්‍රී ලංකාව ලස්සන රටකි.</strong> This is a reference example for the FM Abhaya mapping profile.</p><h2>Important FM Abhaya notes</h2><p>This converter targets the FM Abhaya mapping profile and compatible FM-family usage; it is not a universal converter for every Sinhala legacy font or every historical font revision. Some old documents may contain Windows-1252 characters, custom glyphs or application-specific substitutions. For Unicode-to-FM output, use a compatible FM font in your destination editor. The font itself is not bundled with this website; use a properly licensed copy. Keep your original document and proofread the result before relying on it.</p><h2>Related Sinhala and Unicode tools</h2><div class="link-pills"><a href="/fm-abaya-to-unicode/">FM Abhaya to Unicode</a><a href="/unicode-to-fm-abaya/">Unicode to FM Abhaya</a><a href="/unicode-normalizer/">Unicode Normalizer</a><a href="/utf8-encoder-decoder/">UTF-8 Encoder &amp; Decoder</a></div>
<?php if($slug==='bijoy-to-unicode'||$slug==='unicode-to-bijoy'): ?><p>Bijoy is a legacy Bengali keyboard and font encoding commonly associated with SutonnyMJ. Text typed in this layout is stored as Latin-1-style key sequences, so it may look like English letters and symbols unless the compatible legacy font is applied. Converting it to Unicode Bangla makes the text readable across modern browsers, phones and applications.</p><h2>How to use the Bijoy converter</h2><ol><li>Choose Bijoy → Unicode for text copied from a document that uses the SutonnyMJ font.</li><li>Choose Unicode → Bijoy when preparing Bangla text for a workflow that specifically requires Bijoy-compatible keystrokes.</li><li>Paste the text, select the conversion direction, and convert.</li><li>Review conjuncts, vowel signs, punctuation and numbers before using the result in an important document.</li></ol><h2>Bijoy conversion example</h2><p>The Bijoy sequence <code>Avwg evsjvq Mvb MvB</code> converts to <strong>আমি বাংলায় গান গাই</strong>. This sample is a useful quick check for the expected SutonnyMJ mapping profile.</p><h2>Important Bijoy conversion notes</h2><p>This converter targets Bijoy / SutonnyMJ ANSI mapping. It is not a universal Bengali legacy-font converter and should not be assumed to support other layouts or font-specific encodings. Legacy key sequences can represent Bangla glyphs rather than English letters, so mixed English text may need to be retyped or formatted separately after conversion. For Unicode-to-Bijoy output, use a compatible SutonnyMJ font in the destination application. Keep an unchanged copy of the original text and proofread the converted output.</p><h2>Related Bengali and Unicode tools</h2><div class="link-pills"><a href="/bijoy-to-unicode/">Bijoy to Unicode</a><a href="/unicode-to-bijoy/">Unicode to Bijoy</a><a href="/unicode-normalizer/">Unicode Normalizer</a><a href="/utf8-encoder-decoder/">UTF-8 Encoder &amp; Decoder</a></div>
<?php if($slug==='bamini-to-unicode'||$slug==='unicode-to-bamini'): ?><p>Bamini is a legacy keyboard/font encoding used for Tamil text in older desktop publishing and document workflows. The Bamini converter maps those legacy keystrokes to Unicode Tamil characters. Unicode text can then be searched, copied and shared without requiring the Bamini font. The reverse direction produces Bamini-compatible keystrokes for workflows that still use the legacy font.</p><h2>How to use the Bamini converter</h2><ol><li>Choose Bamini → Unicode for text copied from a document that uses the Bamini font.</li><li>Choose Unicode → Bamini when preparing Tamil text for a legacy Bamini-based application.</li><li>Paste your text and select the conversion direction above the input box.</li><li>Convert the text, then compare the result with the original document before using it.</li></ol><h2>Bamini conversion example</h2><p>The Bamini sequence <code>KUfd;</code> is documented in the source project's example as <strong>முருகன்</strong>. Use it as a quick check that the input font profile and conversion direction are correct.</p><h2>Important Bamini conversion notes</h2><p>Bamini uses keyboard characters and symbols as Tamil glyph codes. Its mapping is not interchangeable with other Tamil legacy encodings such as TSCII, ShreeLipi or Vanavil. Some rare glyph combinations and punctuation may need manual review. English words typed with ordinary Latin letters can be ambiguous in legacy text because the same keystrokes may represent Tamil glyphs; separate or retype mixed English after conversion. For Unicode-to-Bamini output, the destination editor must use a compatible Bamini font. Keep an untouched copy of the original text, especially for legal, academic or archival documents.</p><h2>Related Tamil and Unicode tools</h2><div class="link-pills"><a href="/bamini-to-unicode/">Bamini to Unicode</a><a href="/unicode-to-bamini/">Unicode to Bamini</a><a href="/unicode-normalizer/">Unicode Normalizer</a><a href="/unicode-text-converter/">Unicode Text Converter</a><a href="/utf8-encoder-decoder/">UTF-8 Encoder &amp; Decoder</a></div>
<?php elseif($slug==='preeti-to-unicode'||$slug==='unicode-to-preeti'): ?><p>Preeti is a legacy Nepali font encoding. In older documents, the stored characters may be Latin letters and symbols that display as Devanagari only when the Preeti font is applied. Converting the text changes the underlying characters into Unicode Devanagari so it can be searched, copied and shared more reliably.</p><h2>How to use the Preeti converter</h2><ol><li>Choose Preeti → Unicode for text copied from a document formatted with the Preeti font.</li><li>Choose Unicode → Preeti when preparing text for an older workflow that specifically requires Preeti.</li><li>Paste your text, review the output, then copy or download it.</li><li>For Unicode-to-Preeti output, apply the Preeti font in your destination editor.</li></ol><h2>Important accuracy notes</h2><p>This mapping supports common Preeti keystrokes, common conjuncts and legacy ordering rules. Other Nepali legacy fonts such as Kantipur or Sagarmatha use different mappings and should not be processed with this Preeti profile. Mixed English text may be ambiguous because Preeti uses ordinary keyboard characters as glyph codes. Always compare important names, numbers, punctuation and conjuncts with the original.</p><h2>Preeti conversion examples</h2><p>For example, the legacy sequence <code>g]kfn</code> converts to <strong>नेपाल</strong>. The reverse conversion is intended for documents that will be displayed using the Preeti font.</p><h2>Related Nepali and Unicode tools</h2><div class="link-pills"><a href="/unicode-to-preeti/">Unicode to Preeti</a><a href="/unicode-normalizer/">Unicode Normalizer</a><a href="/unicode-text-converter/">Unicode Text Converter</a><a href="/utf8-encoder-decoder/">UTF-8 Encoder &amp; Decoder</a></div>
<?php elseif($slug==='unicode-normalizer'): ?><p>Unicode text can sometimes represent visually identical characters with different underlying code-point sequences. Normalization applies a standard Unicode transformation so equivalent strings can be compared or processed more consistently.</p><h2>Which normalization form should I use?</h2><p>NFC is a common choice for general text interchange. NFD decomposes characters into base characters and combining marks. NFKC and NFKD also apply compatibility transformations, which can change distinctions such as presentation forms. Choose compatibility normalization only when that behavior is appropriate for your data.</p>
<?php elseif($slug==='utf8-encoder-decoder'): ?><p>UTF-8 stores Unicode text as a sequence of bytes. This tool displays those bytes as two-digit hexadecimal values and can reconstruct text from a valid sequence of UTF-8 bytes.</p><h2>How to decode UTF-8 hex</h2><p>Enter byte values such as <code>48 65 6C 6C 6F</code>, separated by spaces or commas. The input must contain complete hexadecimal byte pairs and form valid UTF-8. Invalid byte sequences are reported rather than silently replaced.</p>
<?php elseif($slug==='remove-invisible-characters'): ?><p>Copied text can contain formatting characters that are difficult to see, including zero-width marks, byte-order marks and other Unicode format controls. They may affect search, matching, layout or text processing.</p><h2>What gets removed?</h2><p>The cleaner targets invisible format characters and selected non-printing marks. It preserves ordinary letters, punctuation, spaces and line breaks. Use the report option first when you want to inspect matching code points before removing them.</p>
<?php elseif($slug==='text-cleaner'): ?><p>Text pasted from documents, websites and spreadsheets can contain inconsistent spacing and blank lines. Select a cleanup action to trim line edges, reduce repeated spaces or remove consecutive empty lines.</p><h2>Review before replacing your original</h2><p>Whitespace can carry meaning in code, poetry, tables and carefully formatted documents. Keep a copy of the source and choose only the cleanup operation that suits your text.</p>
<?php elseif($slug==='unicode-escape-converter'): ?><p>Unicode escape sequences represent characters using hexadecimal code points. This tool supports four-digit <code>\uXXXX</code> escapes and braced <code>\u{...}</code> escapes for code points outside the Basic Multilingual Plane.</p><h2>Escape sequence examples</h2><p>The letter A can be written as <code>\u0041</code>, while many emoji require a braced code-point form such as <code>\u{1F30D}</code>. Decoding validates the code point and does not execute input as code.</p>
<?php elseif($slug==='ascii-converter'): ?><p>ASCII is a 7-bit character encoding with values from 0 to 127. This tool converts basic ASCII characters into decimal values and reconstructs text from valid decimal ASCII codes.</p><h2>ASCII limitations</h2><p>Standard ASCII cannot represent most accented letters, non-Latin writing systems or emoji. If your text includes those characters, use UTF-8 or a Unicode-based workflow instead.</p>
<?php elseif($slug==='text-case-converter'): ?><p>Change the letter case of headings, sentences or pasted text without retyping it. Available options include uppercase, lowercase, title case, sentence case and toggle case.</p><h2>Case conversion and languages</h2><p>Case rules differ between writing systems and languages. This browser-based converter uses the browser's Unicode-aware case functions, but language-specific editorial rules may require manual review.</p>
<?php elseif($slug==='word-character-counter'): ?><p>Check the length of text before submitting a form, preparing a title or editing a document. The analyzer reports words, characters, characters excluding whitespace, lines and paragraphs.</p><h2>How counts are calculated</h2><p>Words are estimated by splitting non-empty text at whitespace. Characters are counted as Unicode code points rather than UTF-16 code units, so many emoji count as one character. Different publishing platforms may use different counting rules.</p>
<?php else: ?><p>Text conversion depends on the encoding or font mapping used by the source document. Unicode represents characters consistently across modern systems, while many older desktop publishing workflows use font-specific character positions. This is why choosing the correct legacy font matters.</p><h2>Before you convert</h2><p>Keep the original document unchanged and compare the output in the application where it will be used. A successful conversion should preserve the intended language, punctuation, spacing and reading order. If the result looks incorrect, check the source font and the exact font version.</p><?php endif; ?>
<h2>Before using the result</h2><p>UnicodeFix processes these ready text utilities in your browser. Review the output before using it in important documents, code, publishing workflows or data imports. Keep your original text as a backup.</p><h2>Related tools</h2><div class="link-pills"><a href="/unicode-text-converter/">Unicode Text Converter</a><a href="/utf8-encoder-decoder/">UTF-8 Encoder &amp; Decoder</a><a href="/remove-invisible-characters/">Remove Invisible Characters</a><a href="/">Browse all tools</a></div></div></section>
